    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d100">

                <pb facs="0180" n="1644" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p2/pg1644"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">792.00/12–1651: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            Embassy in Thailand
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-12-18T18:05:00-05:00">December 18,
                            1951—6:05 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1284. Re instrs requested urtel 1829 Dec
                        16<anchor n="1" xml:id="d100-f1"/> see Dept’s 1242 Dec 10<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d100-f2"/> to effect Emb shld merely ack new situation without
                    making reply an act of recognition in view fact US-Thai relations were not
                    broken while King Phumiphon continued Chief of State.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d100-f1" n="1" xml:id="d100-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in telegram 1329, December 16, the Embassy in Bangkok reported that
                    the British and most other foreign missions in Thailand were on the verge of
                    resuming formal relations with the new Thai Government by exchanging notes to
                    that effect. Chargé Turner wished to know what position the Embassy should
                    adopt. (792.00/12–1651)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d100-f2" n="2" xml:id="d100-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
